616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8ae1369-b7ee-5c00-a777-3fe6f0aa3a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381716} {:answer/id #uuid "6b391104-ba09-582c-a764-3db08a687474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381717} {:answer/id #uuid "0b8606e8-2d30-5d6c-bbb3-aa8426a3b2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381718} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3e489a-837d-5d9c-8b82-1575c46709a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381719} {:answer/id #uuid "50ed8ad0-0200-5320-9567-1a74e669e541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b391104-ba09-582c-a764-3db08a687474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381717} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aacbb1a-6cc8-56de-a3cf-e8097ea08557" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1dd2623-6f10-56c3-88a0-4d1c15254f6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-183501" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(y):$ ${y}^{3}\\ge 0$ and ${y}^{2}<0.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set ${R}_{0}^{+},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(y)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(y)$ is ${R}_{0}^{+}.$\n- $∀y\\in {R}_{0}^{+},\\,P(y).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"${y}^{3}\\ge 0$\" is ${R}_{0}^{+},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${y}^{2}<0$\" is $∅.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={R}_{0}^{+}\\cap ∅=∅.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${T}_{P}\\ne {R}_{0}^{+}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ does *not* coincide with the universal set, the statement $∀y\\in R,\\,P(y)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc087c2-923a-5910-a9ef-14c65af62588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381723} {:answer/id #uuid "a82bf02c-c138-52ea-bedf-aa6d9acfc4bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381724} {:answer/id #uuid "55d75bb9-2dcc-5444-8dd9-16a2e123e059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381725} {:answer/id #uuid "460a515f-8e30-53e1-b2a3-9c9ed3d0dcba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381726} {:answer/id #uuid "d79c1d50-f77c-5ac2-9649-da9e99566897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cc087c2-923a-5910-a9ef-14c65af62588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381723} :answer-field/id #uuid "513be37b-b67b-5495-aa2e-ed37027d1b39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df50f5a8-2783-58fd-a4cd-90489ec7d8e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-183505" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x>0$ or $x\\le 0.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z.$\n- $∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$x>0$\" is ${Z}^{+},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$x\\le 0$\" is ${Z}_{0}^{-}.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={Z}^{+}\\cup {Z}_{0}^{-}=Z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e559b713-e0dc-5119-9e81-6afae77d2e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381730} {:answer/id #uuid "758e76ec-b605-5ae0-b77f-95c41878a815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381731} {:answer/id #uuid "0a1a2f46-9029-5c04-8b12-dc4bfb605f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381732} {:answer/id #uuid "513a5cc1-c39d-50fa-a204-e97f550b4c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381733} {:answer/id #uuid "7affa7f5-9f6c-568e-883a-be6cabd4d82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a1a2f46-9029-5c04-8b12-dc4bfb605f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381732} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d507c6c-26e9-534f-84d7-d4a455d7ca6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9947189b-8991-5f0c-ad01-e90d4c094369" :question/math-academy-id "q-183507" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(z):$ ${e}^{z}<0$ or ${z}^{4}\\le -1.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $R,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $R.$\n- $∀z\\in R,\\,P(z).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"${e}^{z}<0$\" is $∅,$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${z}^{4}\\le -1$\" is $∅.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=∅\\cup ∅=∅.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${T}_{P}\\ne R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ does *not* coincide with the universal set, the statement $∀z\\in R,\\,P(z)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60816447-8e01-558a-98c7-c7d2ea05adac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381737} {:answer/id #uuid "8916a66d-c643-5c2f-bd17-ce73d117d3b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381738} {:answer/id #uuid "2203b41c-ae30-5a22-86e3-3f23ea03e531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381739} {:answer/id #uuid "ab15b8c9-5c02-528d-bc7c-1c3e1595107c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381740} {:answer/id #uuid "050255d5-55c3-5434-b1fb-5676d408b9b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60816447-8e01-558a-98c7-c7d2ea05adac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381737} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bdca389-545f-586b-905f-d9f4acda2d50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "53e303c8-88d9-51db-bc34-ac5638b42f29" :question/math-academy-id "q-183511" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(z):$ $\\frac{z}{2}\\in Q⇒(z+1)^{2}\\ge 0.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Q,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $Q.$\n- $∀z\\in Q,\\,P(z).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$\\frac{z}{2}\\in Q$\" is $Q,$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$(z+1)^{2}\\ge 0$\" is $Q.$\n\nSince $A⇒B≡¬A∨B,$ the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\overset{―}{Q}\\cup Q=∅\\cup Q=Q.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is not empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=Q.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $∀z\\in Q,\\,P(z)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320c4694-1904-5fc7-a324-a4c89f0e3257" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1a68cd3-a209-55d3-a3d8-25a77ff1f9b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381751} {:answer/id #uuid "82ca3bb3-266a-5eb8-8810-df05717789a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381752} {:answer/id #uuid "65ae5aff-2f74-53fe-b4a3-84427d4067fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381753} {:answer/id #uuid "055c76e7-59ee-5b3b-bf61-9c2f432cc842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381754} {:answer/id #uuid "97582ec3-dc87-5beb-8eb8-161a6bef315e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65ae5aff-2f74-53fe-b4a3-84427d4067fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381753} :answer-field/id #uuid "0477c06d-a387-55dd-a1ab-ae56b8769dcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79851a4f-18e1-5d49-91ff-0a7efb6d90a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-183549" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(z):$ $\\sqrt{z}<1$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $N,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(z)$ is $N.$\n- $∃z\\in N,\\,P(z).$" :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=∅.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${T}_{P}\\ne N.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ is empty, the statement $∃z\\in N,\\,P(z)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320c4694-1904-5fc7-a324-a4c89f0e3257" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1c3e13d-dfe9-54ce-bd49-3ad00ea62271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381765} {:answer/id #uuid "720869ce-9907-570c-9524-4464449e67ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381766} {:answer/id #uuid "5894d3c3-41da-538b-8764-e57f7b818aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381767} {:answer/id #uuid "9c11803d-8e21-563f-aa8b-aaa591182679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381768} {:answer/id #uuid "9308c0b8-8805-5989-9517-e7ed3dd40f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1c3e13d-dfe9-54ce-bd49-3ad00ea62271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381765} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fa1dcb4-d3c8-577d-b54e-e234305eda3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f36cab2-ae5b-5390-a225-e9d92c28352a" :question/math-academy-id "q-183554" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ ${x}^{3}<0$ or ${x}^{2}=0$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set ${Z}^{-},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is ${Z}^{-}.$\n- $∃x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,P(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"${x}^{3}<0$\" is the set ${Z}^{-},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${x}^{2}=0$\" is $∅.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={Z}^{-}\\cup ∅={Z}^{-}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}={Z}^{-}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320c4694-1904-5fc7-a324-a4c89f0e3257" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "662c24e6-7d42-57e1-8eb0-87256dabb5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381772} {:answer/id #uuid "856bbb2f-1280-5e6c-b3f4-140c644a21b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381773} {:answer/id #uuid "60d8cc35-c88b-5733-9f8f-48b69b6a7ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381774} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3c1b6c-d3b0-57fb-b1c4-558ac6d76d14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381775} {:answer/id #uuid "334de6a5-1f4c-514e-acc5-e0737160100c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "662c24e6-7d42-57e1-8eb0-87256dabb5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381772} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fa19dbf-ef0b-5a19-95f6-1cf27ea12075" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "79e978ef-4c7c-5a72-a1cc-3c8d675edfbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-183559" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $\\sqrt{x}-2=0⇒{x}^{2}\\in N$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z.$\n- $∃x\\in Z,\\,P(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$\\sqrt{x}-2=0$\" is the set ${4},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${x}^{2}\\in N$\" is $Z∖{0}.$\n\nSince $A⇒B≡¬A∨B,$ the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\overset{―}{{4}}\\cup (Z∖{0})=(Z∖{4})\\cup (Z∖{0})=Z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃x\\in Z,\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}]}
